{
  "id": "q-1226",
  "parts": [
    {
      "part": "problem",
      "original_record": "before",
      "value": "If $C=\\{-3,-1,0,5,10\\}$ and $D=\\{-2,-1,0,1\\}$, then $C∖D=$",
      "original_html_sha256": "97ba918ea05212f6de3e2a13312789e35e5267ecb8398f8814956fca95ad226e",
      "extractor_sha256": "1591183da328a4140792793d2cc113fd535ab5f25bc7f284ceb1b6f251efad18",
      "source_file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071857/repairs/source-provenance-v2/structured-source/source/97ba918ea05212f6de3e2a13312789e35e5267ecb8398f8814956fca95ad226e.html",
      "extraction_errors": []
    },
    {
      "part": "worked_solution",
      "original_record": "history",
      "value": "The set $C∖D$ is the set of all elements belonging to $C$ but *not* $D.$ Note that the elements $-1,0\\in D$ are also in $C,$ so we must remove them. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C∖D & =C-D \\\\  & =\\{-3,-1,0,5,10\\}-\\{-2,-1,0,1\\} \\\\  & =\\{-3,5,10\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "original_html_sha256": "bc93d653669547db18d994a2d6e3d2fdef9e54caef014c0a68912cc0e9266d0f",
      "extractor_sha256": "1591183da328a4140792793d2cc113fd535ab5f25bc7f284ceb1b6f251efad18",
      "source_file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071857/repairs/source-provenance-v2/structured-source/source/bc93d653669547db18d994a2d6e3d2fdef9e54caef014c0a68912cc0e9266d0f.html",
      "extraction_errors": []
    }
  ]
}
